---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import VideoFile from '../../components/VideoFile.astro';

// Set to false until the video files are actually in public/video/ — otherwise
// the player renders a broken source. See public/video/README.txt for the
// ffmpeg commands to compress the recordings and pull a poster frame.
const hasMapDemo = true;
const hasTalkRecording = false;
---

<Base
  title="Affordable housing access"
  description="An interactive map of CHAPA's affordable housing applications: who applies, where they apply from, and how far they search, filterable across demographics, price, and distance."
>
  <article class="section">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="label">2025 · CHAPA · BU Spark! DS701</p>
      <div>
        <h1 class="page-title">Affordable housing access</h1>
        <p class="lede head-lede">
          As part of a collaboration with CHAPA and BU Spark!, I worked with classmates to analyze property data and provide insights about applicant demographics. My primary contribution to the project was an interactive map of affordable housing applications across Massachusetts, showing applicant flow lines, demographic breakdowns, and more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    {
      hasMapDemo && (
        <VideoFile
          src="/video/MapDemoShort.mp4"
          poster="/video/chapa-map-demo-poster.jpg"
          title="Walkthrough of the interactive applications map built for CHAPA"
          caption="Walkthrough of the map, recorded for a client meeting."
        />
      )
    }

    <div class="body">
      <h2 class="section-heading">The map</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The tool is a Folium and Leaflet application over four toggleable
          layers: 109 properties, the applicant origins feeding them, and
          roughly 750 lines connecting the two. Each property is a pie chart
          placed at its location, sized by how many applications it received and
          divided by the racial and ethnic composition of its applicant pool, so
          volume and composition read at a glance without opening anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          The layer that does the most work is the flow lines. Every applicant
          origin is joined to the property they applied to, which turns an
          abstract question — how far do people search for affordable housing? —
          into something you can see. Hovering a flow gives the count from that
          origin, the age distribution, and the demographic breakdown of the
          people it represents. Opening a property gives its details alongside
          its applicant statistics: median age and range, median distance
          travelled and range, and the demographic composition as proportion
          bars.
        </p>
        <p>
          The remaining layers are applicant origins on their own and a price
          heatmap, and there's a filter panel over the whole thing — minimum
          applications, maximum price, maximum distance, maximum age — so the
          map can be narrowed to a question rather than browsed. Filtering
          applies across every layer at once, which is where most of the custom
          JavaScript went; Folium will render the layers, but the
          cross-filtering, the flow highlighting, and the pie markers all had to
          be written on top of it.
        </p>

        <h3>Context</h3>
        <p>
          CHAPA is the umbrella nonprofit for affordable housing and community
          development in Massachusetts, monitoring close to three thousand
          permanently affordable homes. The team's brief for the semester was
          geographic trends, demographic representation, and access disparities
          in the homeownership portfolio: comparing where applicants lived
          against where they applied, measuring the radius of their search, and
          testing how those patterns differed across families, seniors, and
          racial groups. We also looked at whether marketing strategy, listing
          source, or the complexity of the application itself shaped who applied
          at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project ran through DS701 with BU Spark!, which pairs teams with
          external partners for a semester, and was presented at Demo Day in
          December 2025.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {
      hasTalkRecording && (
        <div class="body">
          <h2 class="section-heading">Demo Day presentation</h2>
          <VideoFile
            src="/video/chapa-presentation.mp4"
            poster="/video/chapa-presentation-poster.jpg"
            title="CHAPA affordable housing team presentation at BU Spark! Demo Day"
            caption="Recorded from the audience at BU Spark! Demo Day, December 2025."
          />
        </div>
      )
    }
  </article>

  <style>
    .head-lede {
      margin-top: 1rem;
    }

    .body {
      margin-top: 3.2rem;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
